# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the Groundhog ASAN/UBSAN job
# (.github/workflows/groundhog-ci.yml, nostrc-qp24.15.2).
#
# Only leaks of libraries outside Groundhog whose objects Groundhog cannot
# free itself belong here, each with its bead; a leak in Groundhog code is
# fixed, never suppressed. LSan matches a pattern against every frame of an
# allocation's stack, so each entry names a function whose allocations are
# all part of the leak it describes (an indirect leak below a suppressed one
# carries the same frame). Remove an entry once its bead is fixed.

# nostr-gobject GNostrSubscription (nostrc-jwj0). Groundhog's gnostr scope
# transport (src/relay/gh-relay-gnostr.c) closes and unrefs every
# subscription it makes, yet each one leaks:
#  - the go_context_background() that gnostr_subscription_new() creates for
#    nostr_relay_prepare_subscription() and never releases;
leak:go_context_background
#  - the core NostrSubscription with its contexts and channels, which
#    gnostr_subscription_finalize() hands to a detached
#    nostr_subscription_free_async() thread and abandons;
leak:nostr_relay_prepare_subscription
#  - and what nostr_subscription_free_async() itself allocates for that
#    thread (its cleanup handle and done channel), leaked when the thread
#    has not run by exit: a race, seen under load (nostrc-xbso). Anchored:
#    it allocates nothing on behalf of Groundhog.
leak:^nostr_subscription_free_async$
#  - the filters gnostr_subscription_new() takes ownership of, which
#    finalize drops unfreed (the core subscription only borrows them).
#    Groundhog builds them in one function for this entry alone, so a leaked
#    GNostrSubscription itself still shows.
leak:subscription_filters

# libnostr (nostrc-jwj0): the result channel of the CLOSE frame that
# nostr_subscription_close() writes (nostr_relay_write) is never freed, nor
# the allocation nostr_subscription_dispatch_closed() makes per CLOSED.
# Anchored: a bare "nostr_subscription_close" is a substring of nostr-gobject's
# gnostr_subscription_close, whose frames would then hide a leak in any
# handler reached through that wrapper (W15 review non-blocking #7).
leak:^nostr_subscription_close$
leak:nostr_subscription_dispatch_closed

# libnostr relay writer (nostrc-jwj0): write_operations() answers each write
# with an Error sent into the caller's answer channel and queues the frame
# into the connection's send channel; when the caller or the connection is
# gone first (a relay torn down with a write in flight, e.g. the AUTH or
# EVENT of a publish that just ended), neither is ever freed.
leak:write_operations
# ... and one step earlier (nostrc-xbso): nostr_relay_write() allocates the
# request, its frame copy and the answer channel, keeps a channel reference
# for the writer, and queues the request; a relay torn down with the request
# still queued never frees it (Groundhog's gh-relay-gnostr-write.c releases
# its own reference). A race, seen under load. Blind spot: a caller that
# forgets to unref its answer channel is hidden too.
leak:^nostr_relay_write$
# ... and its error path (nostrc-xbso): when the request cannot be queued,
# write_error() sends a new Error into the answer channel from its own
# thread; a caller that has closed the channel by then (Groundhog's write
# confirmation timed out) never receives it, and the failed send drops it.
leak:^write_error$
